# Pin end-of-line normalization so every checkout is byte-identical across platforms.
#
# Git's own `core.autocrlf` default is false, but Git for Windows installs with it set
# to true -- and that is what the windows-latest runner image ships -- so stored LF
# becomes CRLF in the working tree there. A test that reads a file off the working tree
# and asserts on a "\n" literal *in the content it read* is then red on windows-latest
# while green on ubuntu-latest and macos-latest. That one-platform failure reads like a
# flake, re-running never clears it, and it does not reproduce by default for an author
# on Linux or macOS. It is not hypothetical: the entrypoint-shebang assertion in
# packages/lhremote/src/cli-parity.test.ts failed exactly that way before it was
# rewritten to accept either ending (see #997).
#
# Keep BOTH tokens. `text=auto` is the one that looks droppable and is not -- it is what
# keeps git's binary detection alive on the check-in path. Measured on git 2.50.1 against
# a binary holding CR-LF pairs: `* text=auto eol=lf` stores it byte-identical, while a
# tidied-up `* eol=lf` strips every CR out of it. The tracked SQLite fixture holds no
# CR-LF pair today and so would survive either spelling, but that is luck and it lasts
# only until the fixture is regenerated. `eol=lf` is the other half: it pins the
# working-tree ending whatever a contributor's `core.autocrlf` says.
#
# This is the checkout-level counterpart to `end_of_line = lf` in .editorconfig, which
# governs editors and has never governed what git writes to disk.
* text=auto eol=lf
